      "id": 1,
      "text": "Little Tokyo, founded around the early 1900s, is the cultural heart of Japanese Americans in Southern California.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Tokyo%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first Japanese newspaper in the United States, Rafu Shimpo, began publishing in Little Tokyo in 1903 and still runs today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Tokyo%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Japanese Episcopal Mission of Los Angeles, now Century United Methodist Church, was the first religious institution in Little Tokyo, founded in 1896.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Tokyo%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A chef named Ichiro Mashita invented the California roll at Tokyo Kaikan sushi restaurant in Little Tokyo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Tokyo%2C_Los_Angeles"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early entrepreneurs in Little Tokyo used rotating savings groups called tanomoshiko to fund new businesses in the early 1900s.",
